[0m[0m
The translation had [1mtwo bugs[22m, both in how [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m was used. Three files were modified:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Incorrect control flow in [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m ([38;5;10mblake256.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m function has a single [38;5;10mif[0m block that, when the buffer has leftover data and there's enough new data to fill a block, compresses and then [1mfalls through[22m to the [38;5;10mwhile[0m loop for remaining data. The Rust translation added an [38;5;10melse if[0m branch that handled the case where there wasn't enough data to fill the buffer — but this branch [1mreturned early[22m, preventing the [38;5;10mwhile[0m loop from ever running. This caused a panic when the buffer offset + data length exceeded 64 bytes.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Additionally, the final buffering at the end of the function used [38;5;10ms.buf[..n][0m (always starting at offset 0) instead of [38;5;10ms.buf[left..left+n][0m, and computed [38;5;10mbuflen[0m incorrectly. The C code uses [38;5;10mleft[0m as the offset and computes [38;5;10mbuflen = (left<<3) + datalen[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Removed the [38;5;10melse if[0m branch, made [38;5;10mleft[0m mutable, and corrected the final buffer copy to use [38;5;10mleft[0m as the offset with the proper [38;5;10mbuflen[0m calculation.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: Byte vs. bit length mismatch in callers ([38;5;10mhash.rs[0m, [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m. The standalone [38;5;10mblake256()[0m wrapper correctly converts bytes→bits ([38;5;10minlen * 8[0m). However, the C code in [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m, [38;5;10mhash_message[0m, and all the KAT transcript functions calls [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m directly with [1mbyte[22m lengths (not multiplied by 8). This is the actual behavior that produces the expected KAT digest.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation "helpfully" multiplied all these values by 8 to convert to bits, but this changed the semantics — the hash function would process 8× more data than the C code intended, producing completely different results.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Changed all direct [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m calls in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m and [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m to pass byte lengths (matching the C code exactly), leaving only the [38;5;10mblake256()[0m wrapper to do the [38;5;10m* 8[0m conversion.[0m[0m
